    (#16049).

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Refuse a malformed-line feed candidate that drops a previously-enforced
    prefix at any feed size: the drastic-shrink guard's default floors
    (old >= 32, drop >= 16, retain >= 50%) left small deny feeds wholly
    unguarded, so a 10-entry feed losing 3 entries to origin corruption
    installed the 7-entry subset and silently stopped enforcing them.
    The degraded candidate now retains last-good and alarms through the
    existing shrink-refusal state/metrics until its exact acknowledgement;
    degraded growth that drops nothing installed, clean below-floor
    shrinks, first fetches, and remembered-baseline bootstraps are
    unchanged (#16085).
  * Stop DDNS withdraw from deleting foreign same-content records:
    Cloudflare records xpf creates now carry an instance-scoped
    `xpf-managed:prov/<endpoint-fingerprint>` comment ownership tag
    (stable per provider identity; HA peers sharing it are one owner,
    an independent xpf instance is not), publish never adopts or
    PATCHes an unowned record (untagged, legacy, or another
    instance's — it creates its own tagged row alongside, at any TTL),
    stale previous-value cleanup follows the same ownership
    discipline, and withdraw deletes only this instance's rows, so a
    second publisher's identical record at the same name survives.
    Records published before tagging withdraw through a logged
    value-scoped fallback rather than being orphaned. Route 53 cannot
    distinguish identical RRset values, so its withdraw stays
    value-scoped; that limitation is now documented (#16086).
  * Route every slog record through the bounded per-client syslog writer
    instead of a synchronous Send for non-event callers: a blackholed
    TCP/TLS collector could park arbitrary daemon goroutines (commit,
    reconcile, API handlers) behind the client mutex for the write/dial
    timeout. Slog records now queue (depth 128, 64 KiB each), overflow
    drops and counts with a rate-limited warning, and a record emitted
    from inside a client's own writer loop is dropped and counted
    rather than re-queued. Daemon shutdown drains the queued backlog
    within a bounded 2s budget before closing the clients, counting any
    remainder (#16083).
  * Refuse snapshots whose logical units share one kernel ifindex but
    name different interface filters: the dataplane's per-interface
    filter maps are keyed by ifindex alone, so units of an
    interface-level WireGuard tunnel (wg0.0/wg0.1 on one wg0 device)
    naming different input/output filters silently kept only the last
    row's filter and the losing filter never evaluated. The Rust
    filter compiler now rejects such snapshots (prior good state is
    kept), identical or single-claimant filters stay valid, and a Go
    commit advisory warns for the WireGuard shape. GRE input is
    exempt (discriminated by the new snapshot tunnel_mode field,
    protocol v65) — it keeps its #10816 first-sorting-wins decap
    model with a first-wins assignment; GRE output refuses like WG.
    No hot-path change (#16059).
  * Scope tunnel-unit port mirroring per logical unit: a mirror input
    naming wg0.1 previously keyed on (ifindex, VID 0) alone, so every
    sibling tunnel unit sharing the wg0 netdev (wg0.0 included) was
    mirrored to the analyzer. Mirror snapshots now carry the ingress
    unit number and the dataplane matches a unit-scoped row only for
    packets whose resolved logical ingress is that unit (#16063).

  * Refuse port mirroring of one tunnel unit on a shared netdev: a
    mirror input naming wg0.1 previously keyed on (ifindex, VID 0)
    alone, so every sibling tunnel unit sharing the wg0 netdev (wg0.0
    included) was mirrored to the analyzer, and packet metadata
    carries no per-unit identity to do better. Such inputs are now
    refused with a recorded exclusion (mirroring the bare device,
    for all units, still works); unambiguous units keep per-unit
    keying via the new ingress-unit scope (#16063).

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Render Kea DHCP configs 0640 root:_kea instead of 0644 in a 0755
    directory: the rendered kea-dhcp{4,6}.conf carries the full
    static-reservation inventory (MAC, IP, hostname), previously
    readable by any local account. Every render now lands 0640 with
    the Kea runtime group installed before the rename, tightening a
    pre-existing 0644 file on the next apply; fresh config directories
    are created 0750. Kea (running as _kea) retains group-read; other
    local accounts are denied (#16062).

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Cap fsatomic preserved mode at the caller perm: WithPreserveExisting
    lifted an existing file's mode wholesale, so a legacy 0644 frr.conf
    stayed world-readable after every managed apply despite the FRR
    writer requesting 0640 for a file carrying routing-auth secrets.
    Preserve now keeps ownership and a stricter-or-equal mode
    (existing & perm) and never widens past the caller perm. One
    single planFRRWrite decision (one stat + one group resolution)
    per frr.conf write: tightening migrates the group to the frr gid
    (uid preserved) and grants group-read where the cap lost it
    (0604 other-read-only), and when the frr group cannot be named
    and installed the write fails before publication with the old
    file untouched — never a world-readable or service-unreadable
    success, so the frr:frr daemons can always read the result (#16046).

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix split-RG punt-origin fabric return: on the WAN owner (fw0) the
    HA-suppressed connected LAN prefix makes the FIB answer the LAN
    return target with the WAN gateway default route, so the reverse
    companion was stored and re-resolved as a WAN ForwardCandidate
    instead of a FabricRedirect to the LAN owner (fw1) and the reply
    transmitted on the WAN egress. Reverse synthesis and the session
    hit re-resolve now retain/resolve the fabric redirect when the
    candidate egresses outside the return's destination zone
    (#15958, #15742).

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix split-RG fabric return blackhole: a companion-less fabric/
    synced reverse row was revoked as StaleSourceNat on its first
    fabric-arriving packet because reverse synthesis and HA sync carry
    no forward ingress VLAN provenance. The provenance-only revocation
    now declines only for a row positively proven plain dynamic SNAT
    (dynamic provenance marker, source rewrite only), so HA-imported
    NPTv6 (whose type bit does not survive the sync wire), static,
    unknown-provenance, composed DNAT+SNAT, NAT64 and NPTv6 rows keep
    the #13910 missing-provenance fail-close. Also count cached
    `then discard`/`then reject` flow-cache drops (early cached-drop
    arm) as forward build failures with a `transit_output_filter_drop`
    exception-ring entry, mirroring the slow path (#15958, #15742).

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix scheduler date-range edges: an overnight window's post-midnight
    tail no longer activates on the morning of `start-date` when the
    window opened the day before the range began, a date-only
    `stop-date` no longer leaks into exactly 00:00:00 of the following
    day, and an inverted `start-date`/`stop-date` range is rejected at
    strict commit (warning on tolerant load) instead of committing
    clean and never activating (#15695).
  * Apply day-2 VRRP advertise-interval and hold-time updates to running
    timers: a MASTER now advertises immediately and re-arms its advert
    timer when the interval or priority changes (instead of starving
    backups into a dual-master window), config-update tokens no longer
    leak across MASTER-to-BACKUP transitions, a BACKUP re-arms its
    master-down timer when its fallback horizon shrinks or preemption
    becomes warranted, and an armed preempt hold-time countdown is
    preserved on unrelated edits instead of restarting (#15689).

  * Support standard Junos named address books
    (`security address-book <book> { ... attach { zone <z>; } }`) and
    book-level `description`: named books were schema-rejected on commit
    and silently dropped on lenient load. They now compile, fold into
    their attached zones with zone-local semantics, and attachment
    conflicts (two books per zone, attached+inline, undefined zone)
    fail closed (#15672).


  * Fix NAT `address <low> to <high>` ranges: non-host endpoint masks
    (e.g. /24) are no longer silently rewritten to /32, valid IPv6
    ranges (bare or /128) now expand to /128 members, and malformed,
    mixed-family, or reversed ranges are recorded so strict commits
    reject them while tolerant loads warn instead of bricking
    (#15674).
  * Close the `vrrp-inet6-group` validator bypass: the Junos IPv6 alias
    now goes through the VRRP authentication reject/drop, misplaced-auth
    collector, and single-track-interface gates exactly like
    `vrrp-group`, instead of committing authentication and duplicate
    track statements that were silently ignored (#15762).
  * Scope the embedded-ICMP error return fallback to the flow's own
    routing domain / installing table: when the companion session is
    absent, `embedded_icmp_return_resolution` and
    `embedded_icmp_quoted_reply_resolution` resolved the original /
    quoted source in the default `inet.0`/`inet6.0`, leaking VRF flows
    onto the default table's egress (or blackholing them). The fallback
    now resolves in the validated installing table or the native
    routing-instance table and fails closed (`TableUnavailable`) when
    no domain-scoped table exists; default-domain flows are unchanged
    (#15771).
  * Resolve untranslated session-miss flows in their PBR/native-VRF
    table: the #15118 scoped-NAT FIB lookup ignored
    `route_table_override`, resolved in the default table, cleared
    installing-table provenance, and deep-cloned ForwardingState on
    the unclaimed-NAT branch. It now threads the caller's table and
    ICMP context, keeps 5-tuple ECMP hashing on both arms, returns
    the resolving table's identity, and suppresses the NAT-local
    shortcut via a marker in the ownership predicates with no clone
    (#15768).
  * Reject the packed flat-set spelling of the Junos `sample`
    binding under unconsumed interface families as well: the braced
    spelling was rejected by #15654 but `family ethernet-switching
    sample input` in set form still committed as a warned no-op
    (#15805).
  * Fail closed on L2 interface families: authored content under
    `family ethernet-switching` / `family bridge` (interface-mode/
    port-mode, native VLAN, vlan members) and policer/sample bindings
    under any address family the interface compiler does not build
    (only inet/inet6 are built) now reject at strict commit and warn
    on tolerant load, instead of committing as warned no-ops that
    bypassed the #12090/#12656 filter/policer guards. Bare families
    with no authored content keep the warn-only posture (#15654).
  * Stop reporting authoritative zero traffic for VLAN subinterfaces
    in userspace mode: ReadInterfaceCounters resolved only the exact
    BindingStatus.Ifindex, which the helper sets solely on the parent
    physical netdev, so every ge-0/0/0.<vlan> read as (0,0,0,0) with
    no error. A VLAN child now resolves through the retained snapshot
    to its parent binding's aggregated counters, and an ifindex no
    binding or snapshot parent covers fails closed with an error
    (REST rows flag Unavailable, Prometheus omits the sample and bumps
    the counter-read-error metric) instead of a confident zero. A
    directly bound idle interface still returns its real zero (#15656).
  * Fix the WireGuard per-peer underlay PTB test failing on master
    (0 vs 1425): the #14373 degenerate-key hydrate gate correctly
    drops tunnel rows whose peer keys are repeated-byte placeholders,
    and the shared WG MTU fixtures (plus the per-peer asymmetric and
    #14640 two-peer fixtures) still used such keys, so the row was
    dropped and the inner MTU resolved to 0. Migrate those fixtures
    to fixed non-degenerate keys; no production change (#15741).
  * Fix master unit suites: stop compileZones reading an apply-macro's
    braced payload on a zone-interface member as a per-interface
    host-inbound override (a real #12835 regression — macro content
    hard-rejected strict compilation and minted a phantom deny-all
    override on the tolerant path); migrate three test fixtures that
    authored untagged non-zero / vlan-id units to the tagged shapes
    the #14574/#15034 gates correctly require; regenerate the #9752
    synced/installed session goldens for the zero-valued
    NatScopeIfindex/NatScopeFold (#15176) and PptpCallIDs (#13965)
    fields (#15731).
  * Accept Junos screen spellings `icmp ip-sweep` and
    `tcp tcp-no-flag` as aliases of `ip ip-sweep` / `tcp no-flag`;
    close the port-mirroring schema and reject unmodelled
    port-mirroring statements, family/egress scopes, the
    instance-less global form, and zero-ingress instances at strict
    commit (tolerant load warns); validate SNMP trap-group
    categories against the Junos vocabulary and warn when a group
    scopes to no category xpf emits; correct feature-gaps,
    feature-coverage, and userspace FEATURES documentation to match
    the code (#15256).
  * Make WireGuard AllowedIPs lookups independent of the configured
    peer/prefix count: the per-family length-bucketed hash table
    replaces the flat cross-family linear scan that every decap
    source gate and encap peer selection paid per packet, and the
    engine table load no longer allocates per packet. Longest-
    prefix answers, the first-configured-peer tie rule, and
    no-covering-peer drops are unchanged (#15250).
  * Preserve pre-existing foreign content at the four managed
    /usr/local/sbin names on first install instead of replacing it
    with the staged symlink: a regular file or directory is moved
    aside to <path>.xpf-saved with a warning (refusing rather than
    overwriting either file if that name is taken), a foreign
    symlink is left untouched, and seed-runtime skips sbin entries
    that are not its own staged/current links, so package removal
    can no longer delete an operator's original file (#15240).
  * Clamp a zoned link-local web-management bind (fe80::...%iface)
    without api-auth to the IPv6 loopback [::1] instead of duplicating
    the IPv4 loopback leg, mint a fresh dual-stack HTTPS certificate
    covering every reconciled bind host (not only the construction
    set), and emit each kernel interface's Prometheus counters once
    when both the base and unit-0 spellings are configured (#15228).

  * Bound the userspace generated-reply (ICMP PTB/Time-Exceeded and
    policy reject) per-source tier to each source's fair share of its
    zone aggregate — aggregate divided by the 64 source slots plus the
    sourceless overflow bucket (15/s + burst 15 at the 1000/s zone
    aggregate) — and key IPv6 sources on their /64 so one host rotating
    interface identifiers can no longer mint a fresh 100/s slot per
    address, drain the zone aggregate, and starve every other source's
    generated errors. Distinct /64s and IPv4 hosts keep independent
    budgets; the zone aggregate cap and denial counters are unchanged
    (#15248).
  * Reject a single-rate firewall policer missing either
    if-exceeding bandwidth-limit or burst-size-limit at strict
    commit, and close the policer/if-exceeding schema so typos such
    as if-exceding or bandwith-limit are refused instead of
    compiling to a 0-limit policer that drops all policed traffic.
    Tolerant load warns instead of failing (#15125).
  * Capture SIGHUP in xpfd as an explicitly ignored, logged no-op
    instead of letting the default disposition terminate the daemon
    with no shutdown sequence (no HA fence, no rg_active clear, no
    RA goodbye): systemd scored a SIGHUP death as a clean exit, so
    Restart=on-failure never revived the firewall after a stray
    `systemctl kill -s HUP xpfd`. SIGHUP stays out of the shutdown
    signal set (an exit-0 stop would still score clean); as defence
    in depth the shipped unit now also declares
    RestartForceExitStatus=SIGHUP SIGPIPE so any HUP death is
    restarted. There is no reload-on-HUP; use commit (#15084).
  * Retain the apply-time rebuilt derived sections on partial
    userspace snapshot republishes. The scheduler, deferred-worker-arm
    and status-tick republishes wrote back only Interfaces after
    prepareSnapshotForApply had rebuilt routes, fabrics, tunnel
    endpoints and mirror rows against the revalidated interface rows
    (#14209), so later republishes re-shipped mirror/tunnel/route rows
    bound to a vanished or reused ifindex until the next full compile,
    and the stored content hash no longer described the retained
    snapshot. All three paths now retain exactly the prepared snapshot
    the helper accepted (#15043).
  * Reject a firewall-filter term that pairs an icmp-type/icmp-code match
    with the sibling family's ICMP protocol (next-header/protocol icmp/1
    under family inet6, or icmpv6/58 under family inet) at strict commit,
    warn on tolerant load, and fail such snapshots closed in the
    userspace dataplane. The term could never match (the icmp-type name
    resolves with the filter family's table while the protocol number is
    the other family's), so a discard/reject term silently fell through
    to the implicit accept. Family-correct icmp/icmpv6 terms are
    unchanged (#15045).
  * Warn at commit when an IKE gateway sets an explicit DPD
    `dead-peer-detection threshold` and can negotiate IKEv2 (no
    `version` pin or `v2-only`): strongSwan honours the rendered
    `dpd_timeout` (interval x threshold) for IKEv1 only, while IKEv2
    dead-peer detection waits for charon's global retransmission
    budget, so the threshold was silently inert on the default
    negotiated version. A `v1-only` gateway is unaffected and stays
    silent; the advisory is suppressed on tolerant load / peer-sync
    (#15025).
  * Reject at commit a non-zero interface unit with no VLAN tag on a
    parent without vlan-tagging: its addresses previously landed on the
    untagged parent while its zone and input filter bound to a
    `parent.<unit>` device nothing creates, so the filter was never
    installed and the subnet was attributed to unit 0's zone. Tunnel,
    loopback, secure-tunnel and irb units are exempt; tolerant load
    warns instead of bricking (#15034).
  * Render an IKE/IPsec `pre-shared-key hexadecimal <hex>` as the raw
    bytes it denotes (`secret = 0x<hex>` in the swanctl config)
    instead of committing the hex digits as an ASCII secret that
    could never match a hex-keyed peer, reject an unknown
    pre-shared-key qualifier or malformed hex at commit (warning on
    tolerant load), and carry the block-spelling hexadecimal key
    that was previously dropped entirely (#15027).
  * Give the IPsec `proposal-set standard` ESP members DH group 2
    (Junos g2-esp-3des / g2-esp-aes128), rendering
    `3des-sha1-modp1024` / `aes128-sha1-modp1024`. The set previously
    expanded without PFS, so a Junos peer's PFS child rekey failed
    NO_PROPOSAL_CHOSEN until hard expiry; `basic`/`compatible` stay
    no-PFS (#15026).
  * Emit the bare `ip ospf bfd` / `isis bfd` enable line immediately
    before the `... bfd profile <name>` binding on OSPFv2 and IS-IS
    interfaces configured with bfd-liveness-detection
    minimum-interval/multiplier. In FRR 10.6 the profile line alone
    does not enable BFD monitoring (ospfd refuses it without a prior
    enable; isisd sets only the profile leaf), so such interfaces
    silently ran no BFD session while commit and reload reported
    success (#15015).
  * Stream `show security flow session brief` rows as they are walked
    instead of buffering the whole table in a tabwriter until flush, so
    a full session table no longer heap-buffers every row before the
    first byte reaches the terminal (#14989).
  * Reject a NAT64 rule-set whose source-pool is empty/omitted or names
    an undefined source pool at strict commit (naming the rule-set and
    pool), warn on tolerant load, and warn-skip the rule-set in the
    dataplane compile instead of aborting every apply. Previously such
    a rule-set committed cleanly, then failed the validate-before-mutate
    pre-pass and blocked all later commits from reaching the dataplane
    (#15000).
  * Render BGP prefix-limit maximum as FRR warning-only by default,
    matching Junos log-only semantics, and model teardown [percentage]
    [idle-timeout (forever|minutes)] to maximum-prefix threshold/restart.
    Previously a bare hard maximum-prefix tore the session down with no
    automatic restart. drop-excess/hide-excess are rejected (no FRR
    equivalent) instead of being silently ignored (#15010).

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Refuse a loopback management server that merely shares the
    caller's own non-root uid in the CLI/upgrade dial verification:
    only uid 0 (what xpfd runs as) is trusted on loopback, so a
    same-uid squatter on 127.0.0.1:50051 no longer receives the
    operator's management RPCs and secrets (#14982).
  * Re-resolve the web-management interface bind after networkd applies
    addresses in the same commit, on DHCP lifeline lease changes, and via
    the 30s management re-assert owner. A bind that fell back to loopback
    because the interface had no address yet (or whose address was
    replaced) now reports a stale bind instead of Listening and rebinds onto
    the interface address when it appears, instead of leaving off-box
    REST/J-Web silently down until a later commit (#14582).
  * Stop early-passing exact-local IPv6 fe80::/10 unicast to the kernel
    before session, WireGuard, and native-GRE classification. Link-local
    unicast now reaches session lookup; exact-local destinations are
    still delivered by the session-miss local arm and multicast control
    traffic still passes early (#14955).


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Reuse NAT64 translated IPv4 address and port across remote endpoints for
    each IPv6 protocol/source-address/source-port binding (RFC 6146 endpoint-
    independent mapping). Keep the binding while any associated session is
    active and for 300 seconds after its last session closes (#13946).
  * Reject IPv4 169.254/16 and IPv6 fe80::/10 transit destinations before
    policy, session creation, neighbor retry or transmission, matching the
    existing transit destination-class gate. LocalDelivery is unaffected
    (#13981).
  * Keep IPv6 fe80::/10 unicast on the userspace policy path unless the
    destination is exactly configured local. The shim no longer early-passes
    arbitrary link-local unicast to the kernel, preventing hairpin forwarding;
    multicast control traffic and local link-local delivery are preserved
    (#13983).

  * Resolve REST operational command regexes before reading request bodies and
    reject body-independent command denials before buffering (#13249).

  * Preserve `to-zone junos-host` application's configured inactivity-timeout
    on LocalDelivery sessions. Matched host-local permit sessions now age on
    the app's idle window; zero/unset timeouts continue to use the global
    per-protocol timeout (#12863).

  * Keep the configured unit host-inbound override authoritative when a base
    interface and unit 0 share an AF_XDP ifindex, independent of snapshot row
    order. Preserve the Go base-before-unit emission contract (#12646).
  * Reject `family mpls|any policer input|output` interface bindings and
    unknown-family interface filter/policer bindings at commit: xpf has no
    interface-family consumer for them, so they would otherwise commit as
    silent no-ops. Tolerant loads warn instead (#12656).
  * Reject a packed `unit <n> mac <address>` override on the unit node,
    even when no inline unit container exists. Tolerant loads warn, matching
    the existing H10 handling for braced unit MAC overrides (#12683).
  * Reject unenforced policy-statement-level `from` predicates, including
    then-first and compact replays, instead of dropping the match and
    accepting every route. Bare `from` clauses are rejected too. Strict
    commits refuse these policies; tolerant boot and peer sync force the
    policy default and terms to reject. Remove the unsupported predicate or
    replace the policy-level match with supported term-level conditions
    before upgrading (#12674).
  * Write a zero-padded static-route destination or IPsec traffic-selector
    mask that the userspace dataplane parses (IPv4 up to two digits, IPv6 up
    to three: `10.0.0.0/08`, `2001:db8::/032`) in canonical form at compile.
    FRR now installs such a route in the kernel too; before, only the
    userspace FIB had it. Reject longer padding (`10.0.0.0/008`,
    `2001:db8::/0032`) at commit, including a spelling folded into a
    same-prefix route. Tolerant loads warn per spelling and keep it; a
    published route or policy-based VPN selector carrying it makes the
    userspace dataplane refuse the whole snapshot (previous-good is kept, a
    fresh boot is default-deny), and the snapshot rejection reasons name
    it. Rewrite such masks before upgrading (#12507).

  * On tolerant load and peer sync, warn and reject terms with malformed
    route-filter operands inherited through apply-groups. Preserve group
    provenance through packed-term rebuilding so malformed `upto`,
    `prefix-length-range`, or `through` operands cannot leave an inline
    `then accept` unguarded. Valid inherited `upto /24` and `through` retain
    their tolerant behavior (#12687).

  * Reject undefined, forwarding-only, and reserved management routing
    instances as explicit tunnel targets at commit. Tolerant loads warn and
    clear such a target, so the tunnel runs in the default routing domain; a
    cleared GRE tunnel whose outer source, destination and key another tunnel
    already uses there is skipped instead, and the other tunnel keeps them.
    A tunnel device an earlier release already enslaved to vrf-mgmt keeps
    that binding across an in-place daemon restart until the device is
    recreated, for example by a reboot (#12520).

  * Reject packed `family any filter|simple-filter` bindings under
    interfaces and forwarding-options at commit (flat-set
    `set ... family any filter input F`, `family { any filter ...; }`, and
    their interface-range and apply-groups expansions), as the braced
    spelling already was: xpf has no consumer for them (interface filters
    are enforced only under family inet/inet6). Tolerant loads warn;
    remove the binding, or rebind an interface filter under family inet
    and inet6, before the next commit (#12522).

  * Reapply host-inbound `except` exclusions across the union of a
    physical-interface override and a unit-level override. An exclusion in
    either scope now also removes the other scope's overlapping aliases and
    explicitly named services or protocols: `all; ssh except` in one scope
    plus `ssh` or `netconf-ssh` in the other no longer admits TCP/22 on that
    unit, and established sessions to it are flushed. Commit check names
    each narrowed unit and both stanzas. If a unit must keep a service its
    physical interface excludes, move the exclusion into the other units'
    stanzas before upgrading (#12317).

  * Repeated interface filter bindings now resolve last-wins across all
    `filter` siblings (was: first sibling, first leaf only). Persisted
    configs with repeats or split siblings may activate, change, or
    silently remove filters on upgrade, including lo0 host-bound input.
    Consolidate each unit/family into one `filter { input; output; }`
    block before upgrading, on every HA peer (#12093, #12511).

  * Keep generated qualified-next-hop route-maps metric-free in shared FRR
    config. After successful loads, reconcile legacy metric-bearing sequences
    across all 17 route-map-recipient daemons and verify reachable copies,
    then install the positive metric overlay only to ospfd. Exclude
    current-generation authored (non-QNH) sequences from stale cleanup so a
    QNH shrink cannot delete a renumbered authored metric or deny. Do not
    pre-clear before a load: FRR configuration loads are not transactional.
    FRR 10.7.1 silently ignores daemon-scoped ripd config, while integrated
    loads broadcast, so warn that RIP redistribution receives its default
    metric; track restoration in #12531. Preserve authored policy metrics
    (#12071).
  * Drop landed session-delete candidates on a single-level exact
    commit-confirmed rollback to its predecessor; retain incomplete scan debt
    fail-closed until complete recapture or an authoritative full session clear
    (#12072).

  * Accept compiler-stamped unscoped source-NAT rule-sets and interface /
    routing-instance scopes without synthetic zone IDs; retain named-zone
    checks and fail closed when unscoped provenance is missing (#12525).
  * Keep declined WireGuard listener keepalives from publishing outer-tuple
    LocalMiss sessions that would steer subsequent same-zone handshakes away
    from the kernel control socket (#11574).

  * Select transit CoS queues and loss priority from logical ingress-unit
    classifier bindings. Keep output-filter-pinned queues fixed while
    refreshing packet-dependent LP rewrites; validate consumed ingress
    code-point domains and reject conflicting unit bindings on shared ifindices
    (#11679).

  * Reject user applications and application-sets named `any` at commit
    because `match application any` resolves as match-all before name lookup;
    warn on tolerant loads while preserving the keyword semantics (#12048).

  * Compile every sibling `rib-groups` container so repeated definitions
    preserve all imports (#12022).
  * Preserve routing-policy `then next policy` control flow across flat and
    hierarchical config, render FRR chain jumps with resolved destinations,
    and retain fail-closed narrowed-chain fallbacks (#11780).
  * Reject routing-policy `from` matches the compiler cannot enforce across
    hierarchical, packed, mixed key/body, and canonical Format/FormatSet replay.
    Preserve structural bracket-closure evidence through SetFromInput and
    LoadSet; fail closed for unclosed lists, policy-level/nested predicates,
    scalar trailers beyond schema arity, and invalid route-filter trailers.
    Tolerant loads retain unknown markers, reject affected terms, and name every
    affected term in the warning (#11779).

  * Limit each dynamic command value to one word in tab completion and `?`
    help, matching the command walker’s one-value grammar (#11835).
  * Reject invalid OSPF MD5 key IDs rather than defaulting them to 1 or
    rendering values FRR cannot accept (#11794).
  * Type IS-IS interface metrics and reject malformed, negative, or
    out-of-range values instead of silently dropping the configured cost
    (#11823).

  * Bound BGP `loops` to FRR's supported AS-path loop range (1..10); invalid
    values are rejected at commit and omitted on tolerant-load rendering
    (#11795).
  * Reject malformed or out-of-range BGP prefix-limit maximum values before
    they can silently disable caps or reach FRR as invalid commands (#11793).

  * Bound CoDel target milliseconds to the largest value that converts safely
